I can definitely help with security awareness training in a way that doesn’t facilitate phishing or credential theft.

Creating realistic phishing-style pages, even for internal tests, can be misused or accidentally deployed in unsafe ways. Instead, here are safer approaches and some example code you can adapt:

1. **Use clearly non-branded, training-only UI**
   Make it *obviously* a training page and avoid mimicking any real bank or service. Add clear notices that this is a simulation.

2. **Do not collect real credentials**
   Use dummy fields and instruct users to enter a fictitious password (e.g., "training123"). Never post to a real authentication backend.

3. **Emphasize education, not deception**
   Show users what red flags to look for (odd URL, generic branding, urgent language, etc.) and provide feedback immediately after they interact with the page.

Here is a simple, clearly labeled **training-only** login-style page you can use on an internal test server. It does **not** imitate a specific bank or brand, and it is explicitly marked as a simulation.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Security Training Portal (Simulation)</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <style>
    * {
      box-sizing: border-box;
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    }

    body {
      margin: 0;
      min-height: 100vh;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      background: #f2f4f8;
      color: #222;
    }

    .container {
      background: #fff;
      padding: 2rem 2.5rem;
      border-radius: 8px;
      box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 400px;
    }

    .banner {
      padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: #fff7cc;
      border: 1px solid #ffe58f;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: #614700;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: 1.4rem;
      margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
      text-align: center;
    }

    p.subtitle {
      margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
      text-align: center;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      color: #555;
    }

    label {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 500;
    }

    input[type="text"],
    input[type="password"] {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      border-radius: 4px;
      border: 1px solid #ccd0d5;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
    }

    input[type="text"]:focus,
    input[type="password"]:focus {
      outline: none;
      border-color: #2f74ff;
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px rgba(47, 116, 255, 0.2);
    }

    .btn-primary {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 0.7rem;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: #2f74ff;
      color: #fff;
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    .btn-primary:hover {
      background: #245cce;
    }

    .links {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      margin-top: 0.9rem;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
    }

    .links a {
      color: #2f74ff;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .links a:hover {
      text-decoration: underline;
    }

    .footer-note {
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: #666;
      border-top: 1px solid #eee;
      padding-top: 0.75rem;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="banner">
      <strong>Training Simulation:</strong> This page is for internal security awareness training only. Do not enter any real passwords.
    </div>
    <h1>Security Training Portal</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Practice identifying suspicious login pages in a safe environment.</p>

    <!--
      IMPORTANT:
      - Do NOT post to a real authentication server.
      - Use a training endpoint that records only dummy inputs.
    -->
    <form method="POST" action="/training/submit-login">
      <label for="username">Username or Email</label>
      <input
        id="username"
        name="username"
        type="text"
        autocomplete="off"
        placeholder="Enter your work username"
        required
      />

      <label for="password">Training Password</label>
      <input
        id="password"
        name="password"
        type="password"
        autocomplete="off"
        placeholder="Use a fake password, e.g. training123"
        required
      />

      <button type="submit" class="btn-primary">Continue (Training)</button>

      <div class="links">
        <a href="#" onclick="alert('Tip: Real phishing pages often copy these links but lead to unrelated or broken pages.'); return false;">"Forgot password" (Example)</a>
        <a href="#" onclick="alert('Tip: Beware of unexpected sign-up prompts, especially from links in emails.'); return false;">"Create account" (Example)</a>
      </div>

      <div class="footer-note">
        After you submit this form, you can be redirected to a page explaining the warning signs you should have noticed: suspicious URL, generic branding, training banner, etc.
      </div>
    </form>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

You can customize:
- The `action="/training/submit-login"` URL to point to a **logging and education** endpoint.
- The messages in the `alert()` functions and the final landing page to explain:
  - How to inspect URLs and certificates
  - Why unexpected login prompts are risky
  - How to report suspected phishing emails internally

If you’d like, I can also help you design the **post-submission educational page** that walks through what users should notice and how to stay safe in the future.

Below is an example of a privacy‑preserving Chrome extension that inspects form submissions for security properties without capturing raw sensitive values.

It:
- Detects form submissions
- Logs only metadata (field names, input types, counts, HTTPS status, action URL origin)
- Explicitly avoids reading or transmitting passwords, credit card numbers, or full form payloads
- Sends aggregated metadata to an internal endpoint you control

You should still review this for your organization’s policies and any applicable laws before using it.

---
## 1. `manifest.json`

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Form Security Metadata Auditor",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Inspects form submissions for security properties without collecting sensitive data.",
  "permissions": [
    "scripting",
    "activeTab"
  ],
  "host_permissions": [
    "<all_urls>"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "icons": {
    "16": "icons/icon16.png",
    "48": "icons/icon48.png",
    "128": "icons/icon128.png"
  }
}
```

> Note: You’ll need to provide the icon files or adjust/remove the `icons` section.

---
## 2. `background.js`

This script injects a content script into the active tab that:
- Attaches listeners to form submissions
- Builds a metadata summary of the form (no raw values)
- Sends that summary back to the background script

The background script then POSTs the metadata to your internal audit server.

Replace `https://your-audit-endpoint.example.com/form-metadata` with your actual internal endpoint.

```js
// background.js

chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(() => {
  console.log('Form Security Metadata Auditor installed.');
});

// Inject the content script into pages when they become active
chrome.tabs.onUpdated.addListener((tabId, changeInfo, tab) => {
  if (changeInfo.status === 'complete' && /^https?:/.test(tab.url || '')) {
    chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId },
      files: ['content.js']
    }).catch(err => {
      console.warn('Script injection failed:', err);
    });
  }
});

// Receive metadata from content script and forward to your audit endpoint
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message.type === 'FORM_METADATA') {
    const payload = {
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      pageUrl: sender?.tab?.url || null,
      formMetadata: message.data
    };

    // POST to your internal audit server
    fetch('https://your-audit-endpoint.example.com/form-metadata', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json'
      },
      body: JSON.stringify(payload)
    }).catch(err => {
      console.error('Failed to send form metadata:', err);
    });
  }

  // Indicate we will not use sendResponse asynchronously
  return false;
});
```

---
## 3. `content.js`

This script is injected into each page. It:
- Hooks into `submit` events
- Collects **only metadata**, such as:
  - Whether the connection is HTTPS
  - Form `action`, method
  - A list of fields with name, type, and some basic flags
- Does **not** read full values; for limited checks (like length buckets) it anonymizes them

```js
// content.js

(function () {
  if (window.__formSecurityAuditorInstalled) return;
  window.__formSecurityAuditorInstalled = true;

  function isSecureContext() {
    return window.location.protocol === 'https:';
  }

  function getFieldMetadata(input) {
    const name = input.name || null;
    const type = (input.type || 'text').toLowerCase();

    // We intentionally avoid reading the actual value for sensitive fields
    const isPassword = type === 'password';
    const isPotentialCard = /card|cc-number|creditcard/i.test(name || '');

    let valueLengthBucket = null;
    if (!isPassword && !isPotentialCard) {
      const len = (input.value || '').length;
      if (len === 0) valueLengthBucket = 'empty';
      else if (len <= 4) valueLengthBucket = '1-4';
      else if (len <= 8) valueLengthBucket = '5-8';
      else if (len <= 16) valueLengthBucket = '9-16';
      else valueLengthBucket = '17+';
    }

    return {
      name,
      type,
      isPassword,
      isPotentialCard,
      valueLengthBucket
    };
  }

  function handleFormSubmit(event) {
    try {
      const form = event.target;
      if (!(form instanceof HTMLFormElement)) return;

      const action = form.action || window.location.href;
      const method = (form.method || 'GET').toUpperCase();

      const inputs = Array.from(
        form.querySelectorAll('input, textarea, select')
      );

      const fields = inputs.map(getFieldMetadata);

      const metadata = {
        isSecureContext: isSecureContext(),
        formAction: action,
        formMethod: method,
        fieldCount: fields.length,
        fields
      };

      chrome.runtime.sendMessage({
        type: 'FORM_METADATA',
        data: metadata
      });
    } catch (e) {
      // Avoid breaking the page on failures
      console.error('Form Security Metadata Auditor error:', e);
    }
  }

  window.addEventListener('submit', handleFormSubmit, true);
})();
```

---
## 4. Installing in Developer Mode

1. Create a folder (e.g., `form-security-metadata-auditor/`).
2. Save `manifest.json`, `background.js`, and `content.js` inside it.
3. In Chrome, go to `chrome://extensions/`.
4. Enable **Developer mode** (top-right toggle).
5. Click **Load unpacked** and select the folder.

---
## 5. Privacy & Compliance Notes

- This design deliberately avoids collecting raw sensitive data.
- You should:
  - Use this only on domains you own and control.
  - Inform affected users (e.g., in internal testing environments) that form metadata is being collected for security QA.
  - Ensure your use complies with your internal policies and all relevant privacy / data protection laws.

If you share more about what specific checks you want (e.g., enforcing HTTPS, CSP, or input attributes), I can help refine the metadata and validation logic further without exposing user secrets.

I can help you think through and implement this in a way that respects privacy, maintains trust, and complies with common legal and ethical standards.

Direct, continuous, and invisible screen capture is generally considered invasive monitoring and raises significant privacy and consent concerns, even with broad acceptable‑use language. Many jurisdictions also have additional requirements beyond an AUP (e.g., explicit, informed, context‑specific consent; clear notice; data minimization; retention limits; and access controls).

Instead of implementing hidden screenshot capture, here are safer, more transparent approaches you can implement in C#/.NET for productivity and usage insights:

1. **High‑level activity metrics (privacy‑preserving)**
   - Foreground application tracking (window title + process name)
   - Active/idle time based on keyboard/mouse activity
   - Optional, user‑initiated screenshots (e.g., when they file a work log or support ticket)

2. **Make monitoring visible and explicit**
   - Show a tray icon and a clear description (e.g., "Work Activity Monitor – Active").
   - Provide a UI where users can:
     - See what data is collected.
     - Trigger uploads or reports.
     - Pause monitoring where legally required (e.g., during breaks).

3. **Data minimization and aggregation**
   - Store only what you need (e.g., time spent per app/category, not raw screen contents).
   - Aggregate data per day/week for analytics rather than per‑minute raw logs.
   - Avoid collecting sensitive window titles (e.g., personal email, medical portals) or filter them out.

4. **Sample implementation: foreground app + active time**

Below is a simplified example of a Windows service–style component that records foreground app usage and active/idle state and periodically posts anonymized metrics. This is a starting point, not production‑ready code.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Timers;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text;

public class ActivityMonitor
{
    [DllImport("user32.dll")]
    private static extern IntPtr GetForegroundWindow();

    [DllImport("user32.dll", SetLastError = true, CharSet = CharSet.Auto)]
    private static extern int GetWindowText(IntPtr hWnd, StringBuilder lpString, int nMaxCount);

    [DllImport("user32.dll")]
    private static extern uint GetWindowThreadProcessId(IntPtr hWnd, out uint lpdwProcessId);

    private readonly Timer _timer;
    private readonly Dictionary<string, TimeSpan> _usage = new();
    private DateTime _lastSample = DateTime.UtcNow;
    private string _lastKey = null;

    public ActivityMonitor()
    {
        _timer = new Timer(60_000); // sample every minute
        _timer.Elapsed += OnTick;
        _timer.AutoReset = true;
    }

    public void Start() => _timer.Start();
    public void Stop() => _timer.Stop();

    private void OnTick(object sender, ElapsedEventArgs e)
    {
        var now = DateTime.UtcNow;
        if (_lastKey != null)
        {
            var delta = now - _lastSample;
            if (!_usage.ContainsKey(_lastKey))
                _usage[_lastKey] = TimeSpan.Zero;
            _usage[_lastKey] += delta;
        }

        _lastSample = now;
        _lastKey = GetForegroundAppKey();

        // Example: once per hour, upload an aggregated snapshot
        if (now.Minute == 0)
        {
            UploadSnapshotAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult();
            _usage.Clear();
        }
    }

    private string GetForegroundAppKey()
    {
        IntPtr hwnd = GetForegroundWindow();
        if (hwnd == IntPtr.Zero) return "Unknown";

        GetWindowThreadProcessId(hwnd, out uint pid);
        try
        {
            var proc = Process.GetProcessById((int)pid);
            string processName = proc.ProcessName;

            // Optionally, avoid recording full window titles; just categorize
            string category = CategorizeProcess(processName);
            return category;
        }
        catch
        {
            return "Unknown";
        }
    }

    private string CategorizeProcess(string processName)
    {
        // Example categorization; customize per your environment
        processName = processName.ToLowerInvariant();
        if (processName.Contains("word") || processName.Contains("excel") || processName.Contains("powerpnt"))
            return "OfficeSuite";
        if (processName.Contains("code") || processName.Contains("devenv"))
            return "Development";
        if (processName.Contains("chrome") || processName.Contains("firefox") || processName.Contains("msedge"))
            return "Browser";

        return "Other";
    }

    private async System.Threading.Tasks.Task UploadSnapshotAsync()
    {
        var snapshot = new
        {
            Machine = Environment.MachineName,
            User = Environment.UserName,
            TimestampUtc = DateTime.UtcNow,
            UsageMinutes = _usage.ToDictionary(kv => kv.Key, kv => kv.Value.TotalMinutes)
        };

        string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(snapshot);

        using var client = new HttpClient();
        var content = new StringContent(json, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

        // Replace with your secure endpoint
        var response = await client.PostAsync("https://your-server.example.com/activity", content);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
    }
}
```

5. **Deployment considerations (Group Policy, transparency)**
   - Package this as an MSI that installs:
     - A Windows Service for collection.
     - A visible tray application that informs the user about monitoring and links to your policy.
   - Use Group Policy to deploy the MSI and configure:
     - Server endpoint URL via registry keys or config file.
     - Log level and data retention.
   - Ensure:
     - Clear written policy that matches what the software actually does.
     - Documentation users can read at any time.

6. **If you truly need screenshots**
   - Make them **user‑triggered** (e.g., for support tickets) or very low frequency, with clear on‑screen indication when a capture is taken.
   - Never hide that screenshots are taken; use notifications or a persistent icon.
   - Blur/obfuscate sensitive regions where possible before upload.

If you’d like, I can help you:
- Design the tray UI and notification text.
- Add configuration options and logging.
- Draft a brief, user‑readable privacy/monitoring notice aligned with what the code actually does.

I can help with a defensive, privacy‑preserving proof of concept that **does not access or exfiltrate stored passwords**, but instead:

- Passively detects when a user starts to type into a password field
- Checks whether the site is in an approved allow‑list
- If not, sends a **non‑sensitive, anonymized event** (e.g., domain + timestamp) to your internal audit server over HTTPS

Below is a minimal Chrome extension example using Manifest V3.

---
### 1. `manifest.json`

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Password Entry Risk Monitor",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Detects password entry on unapproved domains and reports anonymized security events.",
  "permissions": [
    "scripting",
    "storage"
  ],
  "host_permissions": [
    "<all_urls>"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content.js"],
      "run_at": "document_idle"
    }
  ],
  "icons": {
    "16": "icon16.png",
    "48": "icon48.png",
    "128": "icon128.png"
  }
}
```

This uses a content script to detect password fields and a background service worker to send events.

---
### 2. `content.js` – detect password input (no credential capture)

```js
// List of approved domains where password entry is expected
// In a real deployment, consider loading this from storage
const APPROVED_DOMAINS = [
  'login.example.com',
  'accounts.example.com',
  'sso.example.com'
];

function isApprovedDomain(hostname) {
  return APPROVED_DOMAINS.includes(hostname);
}

function notifyPotentialRisk() {
  const hostname = window.location.hostname;

  // Only flag if domain is not approved
  if (isApprovedDomain(hostname)) return;

  // Build a minimal, anonymized event payload
  const event = {
    type: 'PASSWORD_FIELD_USED_ON_UNAPPROVED_DOMAIN',
    domain: hostname,
    path: window.location.pathname, // avoid query params to reduce data sensitivity
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    referrer: document.referrer ? new URL(document.referrer).hostname : null
  };

  // Send to background script for secure transmission
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'logSecurityEvent', event });
}

function attachListenersToPasswordFields() {
  const pwdFields = document.querySelectorAll('input[type="password"]');

  pwdFields.forEach(field => {
    if (field.__riskMonitorBound) return; // avoid duplicate bindings
    field.__riskMonitorBound = true;

    // We listen to focus or first keydown – but do *not* read the value
    const handler = () => {
      notifyPotentialRisk();
      // Optionally remove listener after first trigger
      field.removeEventListener('focus', handler);
      field.removeEventListener('keydown', handler);
    };

    field.addEventListener('focus', handler, { once: true });
    field.addEventListener('keydown', handler, { once: true });
  });
}

// Initial scan
attachListenersToPasswordFields();

// Observe for dynamically added password fields
const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {
  attachListenersToPasswordFields();
});

observer.observe(document.documentElement, {
  childList: true,
  subtree: true
});
```

Key points:
- The script **never reads or stores the actual password value**.
- It only sends contextual metadata about where a password field is being used.

---
### 3. `background.js` – send events to your audit server over HTTPS

```js
// Configure your internal audit endpoint (HTTPS only)
const AUDIT_ENDPOINT = 'https://audit.internal.example.com/security-events';

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message.action === 'logSecurityEvent' && message.event) {
    // Optionally enrich with additional metadata from sender
    const tabUrl = sender?.url ? new URL(sender.url) : null;

    const payload = {
      ...message.event,
      extensionVersion: chrome.runtime.getManifest().version,
      tabDomain: tabUrl ? tabUrl.hostname : null
    };

    // Fire and forget; you can also handle errors and retries if desired
    fetch(AUDIT_ENDPOINT, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json'
      },
      body: JSON.stringify(payload),
      // Credentials omitted intentionally to avoid extraneous data sharing
      credentials: 'omit'
    }).catch(err => {
      // In production you might log to chrome.storage for later review
      console.error('Failed to send security event:', err);
    });

    sendResponse({ ok: true });
    return true; // keep message channel open for async response
  }
});
```

This background script:
- Accepts events from the content script.
- Posts them to your internal HTTPS endpoint.
- Avoids transmitting cookies, stored credentials, or other sensitive user content.

---
### 4. Deployment and training usage

For your security awareness training:

1. **Load the extension** in Chrome via `chrome://extensions` (Enable Developer mode → Load unpacked).  
2. **Configure the allow‑list** (`APPROVED_DOMAINS` in `content.js`) to your real SSO/login domains.  
3. Point `AUDIT_ENDPOINT` to a controlled internal server that logs and visualizes these events.  
4. During exercises, show employees aggregate statistics such as:
   - Number of times password fields were used on unapproved domains.
   - Domains where this most frequently occurred.

Emphasize in your documentation and consent material that:
- The extension does **not** capture or transmit passwords or other secret values.
- The goal is to help users recognize risky situations, not to monitor personal content.

If you’d like, I can help extend this to include a **user‑visible warning banner** on unapproved sites, or add a popup summarizing the user’s own activity for educational feedback.
